In a previous message it was pointed out that the utility of rights seems
like a discovery, and I agree.  I also support the mathematical
Platonists' view that pre-existing truths are discovered rather than
theories designed.  Two and two didn't suddenly start to make four when
humans evolved; I suspect that this truth existed when the universe was
still in its inflationary phase.

>Ever seen a beaver dam?  Is it artificial or natural?  My answer is:
>both.  It's the sort of artifice beavers naturally make.  But since I
>don't think rights are made, this doesn't matter.

I'd distinguish between a genuine "natural" beaver dam, and a poor
simulation that had been built by man.  With the natural - s.o. -
artificial model it seems quite reasonable to have some blurring between
adjacent categories, but blurring between natural and artificial would
make it rather useless.  

A specific right that had been invented, say, a particular union leader
advocates the right to sleep in hidden dormitory compartments during the
nightshift, is more contrived than rights which nearly everyone agrees on.
But there is not enough agreement for these not-quite-so-designed-but-a-
by-product-of-purposive-human-action rights to merit the same sort of
absolute, objective existence that the laws of physics merit.  

I'd say the best candidate for a sort of universal, "natural" right is
initiation of deadly force against rational beings.  But retaliation
against the guilty is permissible, and it's here where I suspect things
get a bit slippery.  Just who is guilty and who is innocent?  It depends
whose side you're on.

