On 26 Feb 1994, Reilly Jones wrote:

> While there are many falsities, there is only one truth.  This attempt to define
> truth as "more certain" rather than as an exact match of objective reality is
> what constitutes the intellectual and moral nihilism that destroyed Greek
> civilization and modern Western civilization.

There may indeed be only one truth. Do you own it? Do you possess it? Do 
you know what it is? Can you give it to me?

PCR is about our asymptotic approach to that one truth. In fact, I happen 
to believe that there is only one truth. I do not believe that you or I 
or anyone else knows it in full. Furthermore, I think it unlikely that 
any of us ever will. To know the whole truth would require more bits than 
a human-sized brain can hold. It probably requires more bits than a 
Jupiter-sized brain can hold. Thus the truth that we can hold in our 
heads and use is necessarily imperfect, and only asymptotically 
approaches the mystical One Truth that you like to talk about, and that I 
acknowledge does exist, but is not of much practical use to us.

Certainty is not a trait of the One Truth. Certainty is a trait of ideas 
that we hold in our heads. There is no question that you are more certain 
of the state of your immediate surroundings than you are of, say General 
Relativity. There is no question that we are more certain of General 
Relativity than we were certain of Newtonian Gravitation before 1910. 
Truth may not be relative, but certainty is.

> Eric again: <How do you access [reality] infallibly?
> 
> You don't, and this is an infallible statement of reality.

<some context omitted>

